  "subtitle": "A folding pocket computer with 16 touch zones embedded directly in the LCD — one of the earliest touch-sensitive handheld displays",
  "why_scout_cares": "The Casio PB-1000 put a resistive touch digitizer directly on top of a pocket computer's LCD in 1986 (Japan) / 1987 (international) — years before touchscreens became common on handheld devices. The 4×4 matrix of 16 fixed touch zones rendered as on-screen keys whose functions changed depending on mode: scientific calculation, menu navigation, text editing, or program selection. This was not a modern capacitive touchscreen but a glass-glass resistive panel that the CPU polled for X/Y coordinates, mapping them onto a fixed grid. The folding clamshell design (LCD lifts up, keyboard beneath) was also unusual. Combined with a custom Hitachi HD61700 CPU and an architecture that let users program in both BASIC and assembly language, the PB-1000 represents a brief, strange moment when pocket computing experimented with direct screen interaction before styluses and capacitive touch won. The museum has no artifact demonstrating early embedded resistive touch in a pocket format.",
  "search_terms": ["Casio PB-1000", "pocket computer", "touch screen", "resistive touch", "Hitachi HD61700"],
