    "title": "Casio PB-1000",
    "year": "1987",
    "by": "Casio Computer Co., Ltd. (Japan)",
    "tags": ["HCI", "Input", "Touchscreen", "Portable", "Programming"],
    "overview": "The Casio PB-1000 was a folding pocket computer released by Casio in 1986 in Japan and 1987 internationally. It was Casio's first pocket computer with a touch-sensitive display: a glass-glass resistive digitizer atop a 192×32 pixel monochrome LCD, organized as a 4×4 matrix of 16 touch zones. The touch zones functioned as context-sensitive soft keys — their behavior changed depending on mode, serving as calculation shortcuts, menu navigation, text editing commands, or program selectors. Under the lift-up display sat a full QWERTY keyboard with 64 keys plus 13 dedicated capacitive touch keys.\n\nThe PB-1000 ran on a custom Hitachi HD61700 8-bit CMOS CPU clocked at ~910 kHz, with 8 KB of internal RAM (4 KB user-accessible, expandable to 40 KB via an RP-32 RAM card) and 32 KB of system ROM. It was programmable in Casio's C61 BASIC dialect and HD61700 assembly language via a built-in assembler. A Japan-only educational variant (PB-1000C) replaced assembly with CASL for the COMET educational simulator used in Japanese IT certification exams. Peripherals included the FA-7 cassette/RS-232/printer interface and the rare MD-100 3.5\" floppy disk drive. While not a mass-market hit, the PB-1000 cultivated a dedicated enthusiast community that continues to document it — Piotr Piatek reverse-engineered the HD61700 CPU and built an emulator, Erich Babitsch published a fully commented ROM disassembly, and multiple websites preserve its history in English, German, Japanese, and French.",
    "deepDive": [
      {
        "heading": "The Embedded Resistive Touch Panel",
        "text": "The PB-1000's touch system used a glass-glass resistive digitizer placed on top of the reflective LCD. Two glass layers with transparent conductive coatings were separated by microdot spacers; pressing a touch zone brought the layers into contact, and the CPU polled the resulting X/Y coordinates, mapping them to the 4×4 grid of fixed zone positions. The touch keys were rendered on the LCD as graphical soft keys whose labels and functions changed with context. This was not a capacitive touchscreen (which detects changes in electrical field from a finger's proximity) but a pressure-sensitive resistive overlay — the same fundamental principle used in early ATM and POS touchscreens, miniaturized into a pocket format years before resistive touch became common on PDAs."
      },
      {
        "heading": "The Folding Clamshell Design",
        "text": "The PB-1000 used a distinctive folding design: the LCD display lifted up from the keyboard base on a hinge, revealing the full QWERTY keyboard underneath. Closed, it measured 187 × 97 × 24 mm and weighed approximately 435g with batteries. The LCD displayed 32 characters × 4 lines of text (with a virtual 32×8 screen accessible via scrolling). Below the main LCD, a row of 13 dedicated capacitive touch keys provided quick access to common functions — separate from the 16 resistive touch zones on the main display."
      },
      {
        "heading": "A Programmer's Pocket Computer",
        "text": "The PB-1000 was unusually programmable for a pocket device of its era. It ran Casio's C61 BASIC (based on JIS C6207) with extensive math, trigonometric, and statistical functions. Memory was managed as a 'virtual disk' — multiple BASIC programs, assembly programs, and text files could coexist in RAM. More remarkably, it included a built-in HD61700 assembler: users could type assembly mnemonics directly on the device, 'compile' (assemble) the code, and execute it. The community later reverse-engineered the entire HD61700 instruction set (Casio never publicly released it), developed a Forth implementation (PB_Forth), and built cross-assemblers and emulators."
      },
      {
        "heading": "A Thriving Enthusiast Community",
        "text": "Despite its commercial obscurity, the PB-1000 has one of the richest post-market documentation efforts of any pocket computer. Piotr Piatek (Poland) reverse-engineered the HD61700 CPU, built a Windows emulator, and designed modern USB and solid-state storage replacements. Erich Babitsch (Germany) published 'PB-1000 Intern,' a fully commented ROM disassembly. Andreas Wichmann maintained a comprehensive homepage. Jun Amano (Japan) runs 'PB-1000 Forever.' Hans-Jürgen Böling published a German-language program collection. This community labor ensures the PB-1000 is one of the best-documented obscure computing platforms of its era."
      }
    ],
